AMC.add({"comp": "amc12", "id": "2025A", "label": "2025 A", "problems": [{"q": "<p> Andy and Betsy both live in Mathville. Andy leaves Mathville on his bicycle at <span class=\"tex\">1{:}30</span>, traveling due north at a steady <span class=\"tex\">8</span> miles per hour. Betsy leaves on her bicycle from the same point at <span class=\"tex\">2{:}30</span>, traveling due east at a steady <span class=\"tex\">12</span> miles per hour. At what time will they be exactly the same distance from their common starting point?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3{:}30</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3{:}45</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4{:}00</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4{:}15</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4{:}30</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">t</span> be the number of hours after <span class=\"tex\">1{:}30</span>. Then, if we represent Andy's distance as <span class=\"tex\">d_A</span> and Betsy's distance as <span class=\"tex\">d_B</span>, we get</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">d_A=8t\\qquad d_B=12(t-1)</span></p><p>Thus, for these to be equal, we set <span class=\"tex\">8t=12(t-1)=12t-12</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">4t=12</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">t=3</span>. This means that they reach this equidistant point <span class=\"tex\">3</span> hours after <span class=\"tex\">1{:}30</span>, or at <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)~4:30}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-1"}, {"q": "<p> A box contains <span class=\"tex\">10</span> pounds of a nut mix that is <span class=\"tex\">50</span> percent peanuts, <span class=\"tex\">20</span> percent cashews, and <span class=\"tex\">30</span> percent almonds. A second nut mix containing <span class=\"tex\">20</span> percent peanuts, <span class=\"tex\">40</span> percent cashews, and <span class=\"tex\">40</span> percent almonds is added to the box resulting in a new nut mix that is <span class=\"tex\">40</span> percent peanuts. How many pounds of cashews are now in the box?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3.5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4.5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>Let the weight of the second mix be <span class=\"tex\">x</span> pounds.</p>\n<p>We start by finding the equation for the number of peanuts. We get</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Peanuts from first mix} &amp;= 10(0.50) = 5, \\\\\n\\text{Peanuts from second mix} &amp;= x(0.20) = 0.2x.\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Therefore, in the final mixture of <span class=\"tex\">10+x</span> pounds, we know that peanuts are <span class=\"tex\">40\\%</span> of the mixture, so we can setup the following equation to get:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{5 + 0.2x}{10 + x} = 0.4</span></p><p>We can solve the equation to get <span class=\"tex\">x=5</span>.</p>\n<p>Now, let's find the number of cashews in the final mixture. We know</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Cashews from first mix} &amp;= 10(0.20) = 2, \\\\\n\\text{Cashews from second mix} &amp;= 5(0.40) = 2, \\\\\n\\text{Total cashews} &amp;= 2 + 2 = 4.\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>so the final mixture contains <span class=\"tex\">4</span> pounds of cashews, for an answer of <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)~4}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-2"}, {"q": "<p> A team of students is going to compete against a team of teachers in a trivia contest. The total number of students and teachers is <span class=\"tex\">15</span>. Ash, a cousin of one of the students, wants to join the contest. If Ash plays with the students, the average age on that team will increase from <span class=\"tex\">12</span> to <span class=\"tex\">14</span>. If Ash plays with the teachers, the average age on that team will decrease from <span class=\"tex\">55</span> to <span class=\"tex\">52</span>. How old is Ash?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">28</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">29</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">30</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">32</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">33</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>\nLet <span class=\"tex\">s</span> = number of students and <span class=\"tex\">t</span> = number of teachers. Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">s + t = 15</span></p><p>We can compute the average age of the students as follows:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{12s + A}{s + 1} = 14 \\Rightarrow 12s + A = 14s + 14 \\Rightarrow A = 2s + 14\\tag{1}</span></p><p>and the average age of the teachers:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{55t + A}{t + 1} = 52 \\Rightarrow 55t + A = 52t + 52 \\Rightarrow A = 52 - 3t\\tag{2}</span></p><p>Since we have <span class=\"tex\">2</span> values for <span class=\"tex\">A</span> in <span class=\"tex\">(1)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(2)</span>, we can equate them to get</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">2s + 14 = 52 - 3t \\Rightarrow 2s + 3t = 38\\tag{3}</span></p><p>From <span class=\"tex\">s + t = 15</span>, <span class=\"tex\">t=15-s</span>. We can substitute this into <span class=\"tex\">(3)</span> to get</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">2s + 3(15 - s) = 38 \\Rightarrow 2s + 45 - 3s = 38 \\Rightarrow -s = -7 \\Rightarrow s = 7, t=15-s=8</span></p><p>Using <span class=\"tex\">(1)</span>, we get</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">A = 2s + 14 = 2(7) + 14 = 28</span></p><p>which gives us an answer of <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)~28}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-3"}, {"q": "<p> Agnes writes the following four statements on a blank piece of paper.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At least one of these statements is true.</li>\n<li>At least two of these statements are true.</li>\n<li>At least two of these statements are false.</li>\n<li>At least one of these statements is false.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each statement is either true or false. How many false statements did Agnes write on the paper?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">0</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>\nLet the four statements be <span class=\"tex\">S_1,S_2,S_3,S_4</span> in order, and let <span class=\"tex\">T</span> be the number of true statements and <span class=\"tex\">F</span> the number of false ones, so <span class=\"tex\">T+F=4</span>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>If <span class=\"tex\">T = 0</span>, then all are false. But then <span class=\"tex\">S_3</span> says “at least two of these statements are false,” which would be true. Contradiction. So <span class=\"tex\">T \\ne 0</span>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If <span class=\"tex\">T = 4</span>, then all are true. But then <span class=\"tex\">S_3</span> (“at least two are false”) and <span class=\"tex\">S_4</span> (“at least one is false”) would both be false. Contradiction.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If <span class=\"tex\">T = 2</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">F = 2</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">S_3</span> (“at least two are false”) is true, and <span class=\"tex\">S_4</span> (“at least one is false”) is also true, and <span class=\"tex\">S_1</span> (“at least one is true”) is true as well. That gives at least three true statements, so <span class=\"tex\">T \\ge 3</span>, a contradiction.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If <span class=\"tex\">T = 1</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">F = 3</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">S_1</span> (“at least one is true”) must be true. Since <span class=\"tex\">F = 3</span>, <span class=\"tex\">S_3</span> (“at least two are false”) is also true, giving at least two true statements, so <span class=\"tex\">T \\ge 2</span>, a contradiction.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The only remaining possibility is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">T=3,\\quad F=1.</span></p><p>Indeed, if three statements are true and one is false, then <span class=\"tex\">S_1</span>, <span class=\"tex\">S_2</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">S_4</span> are true, and <span class=\"tex\">S_3</span> is false, which is consistent.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the number of false statements Agnes wrote is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)~1}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-4"}, {"q": "<p> In the figure below, the outside square contains infinitely many squares, each of them with the same center and sides parallel to the outside square. The ratio of the side length of a square to the side length of the next inner square is <span class=\"tex\">k</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">0&lt;k&lt;1</span>. The spaces between squares are alternately shaded, as shown in the figure (which is not necessarily drawn to scale).</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"> <img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2025A/amc10_13q.png\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>The area of the shaded portion of the figure is <span class=\"tex\">64\\%</span> of the area of the original square. What is <span class=\"tex\">k?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{16}{25}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{4}{5}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>\nLet the side length of the largest (outer) square be <span class=\"tex\">1</span>.<br/>\nEach successive inner square has side length multiplied by a factor of <span class=\"tex\">k</span> (<span class=\"tex\">0&lt;k&lt;1</span>), so the side lengths are</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1,\\ k,\\ k^2,\\ k^3,\\ k^4,\\ \\dots</span></p><p>and the corresponding areas are</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1,\\ k^2,\\ k^4,\\ k^6,\\ k^8,\\ \\dots</span></p><p>The shaded area, which are the rings, are just the difference between consecutive squares. For example, the first ring is <span class=\"tex\">1-k^2</span>, second <span class=\"tex\">k^4-k^6</span>, and so on.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2025A/amc10_13s.png\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Thus the total shaded area (as a fraction of the area of the largest square) is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1 - k^2 + k^4 - k^6 + k^8 - k^{10} + \\dots</span></p><p>This is an infinite geometric series with first term <span class=\"tex\">1</span> and common ratio <span class=\"tex\">-k^2</span>, so</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1 - k^2 + k^4 - k^6 + k^8 - k^{10} + \\dots = \\dfrac{1}{1 + k^2}.</span></p><p>We are told that this shaded portion is <span class=\"tex\">64\\%</span> of the area of the original square, so</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp; 1 - k^2 + k^4 - k^6 + k^8 - k^{10} + \\dots = \\dfrac{1}{1 + k^2} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow &amp;\\quad \\dfrac{1}{1 + k^2} = \\dfrac{64}{100} = \\dfrac{16}{25} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow &amp;\\quad 16k^2 = 9 \\Rightarrow k = \\dfrac{3}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Therefore, <span class=\"tex\">k</span> = <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}~\\dfrac{3}{4}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-5"}, {"q": "<p> Six chairs are arranged around a round table. Two students and two teachers randomly select four of the chairs to sit in. What is the probability that the two students will sit in two adjacent chairs and the two teachers will also sit in two adjacent chairs?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{1}{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{1}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{2}{9}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{3}{13}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{1}{4}</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>\nIf we look around the circle, we can seat the people in <span class=\"tex\">6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3</span> total ways (with full distinguishability). However, if our condition is met, we will first seat the teachers in <span class=\"tex\">6\\cdot 2</span> ways (the second teacher must occupy one of the two adjacent spots). Then, the remaining students can choose their seats in <span class=\"tex\">6</span> ways (there are <span class=\"tex\">4</span> chairs left, so there are <span class=\"tex\">3</span> pairs of adjacent seats, and <span class=\"tex\">2</span> orders for them to sit in). Thus our overall probaility is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{6\\cdot 2\\cdot 6}{6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3}=\\dfrac 15</span></p><p>which gives us an answer of <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}~\\frac{1}{5}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-6"}, {"q": "<p> In a certain alien world, the maximum running speed <span class=\"tex\">v</span> of an organism is dependent on its number of toes <span class=\"tex\">n</span> and number of eyes <span class=\"tex\">m</span>. The relationship can be expressed as <span class=\"tex\">v=k n^{a} m^{b}</span> centimeters per hour, where <span class=\"tex\">k,a,b</span> are integer constants. In a population where all organisms have <span class=\"tex\">5</span> toes, <span class=\"tex\">\\log v=4+2\\log m</span>; and in a population where all organisms have <span class=\"tex\">25</span> eyes, <span class=\"tex\">\\log v=4+4\\log n</span> (base <span class=\"tex\">10</span>). What is <span class=\"tex\">k+a+b?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">20</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">21</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">22</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">23</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">24</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>\nNote that <span class=\"tex\">\\log v = \\log k + \\log n^a + \\log m^b = \\log v = \\log k + a\\log n + b\\log m</span>.</p>\n<p>Plugging into the first equation with <span class=\"tex\">n=5</span> and the second equation with <span class=\"tex\">m = 25</span>:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\log k + a\\log 5 + b\\log m = 4 + 2\\log m \\\\\n\\log k + a\\log n + b\\log 25 = 4 + 4\\log n</span></p><p>As the equations must be true no matter how <span class=\"tex\">n</span> and <span class=\"tex\">m</span> vary, the <span class=\"tex\">\\log n</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\log m</span> terms must cancel out in each equation respectively. This gives <span class=\"tex\">a=4</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b=2</span>.</p>\n<p>Substituting into the first equation gives:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\log k + 4\\log 5 + 2\\log m = 4 + 2\\log m \\rightarrow \\log k = 4 - 4\\log 5 = 4(\\log 10 - \\log 5) = 4\\log 2 = \\log 16</span></p><p>So <span class=\"tex\">k + a + b</span> = <span class=\"tex\">16 + 4 + 2</span> = <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)~22}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-7"}, {"q": "<p> Pentagon <span class=\"tex\">ABCDE</span> is inscribed in a circle, and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle BEC=\\angle CED=30^{\\circ}</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{AC}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{BD}</span> intersect at point <span class=\"tex\">F</span>, and suppose that <span class=\"tex\">AB=9</span> and <span class=\"tex\">AD=24</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">BF?</span><br/>\n</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{57}{11}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{59}{11}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{60}{11}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{61}{11}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{63}{11}</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2025A/amc12_8s.png\" width=\"400\"/></div>\n<p>Note that as <span class=\"tex\">\\angle BEC</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle CED</span> are angles inscribed in arcs <span class=\"tex\">\\overset{\\frown}{BC}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overset{\\frown}{CD}</span>, so thus <span class=\"tex\">\\angle BAC=\\angle CAD=30^\\circ</span>.</p>\n<p>This means that <span class=\"tex\">AC</span> is an angle bisector of <span class=\"tex\">\\angle BAD</span>, so we can apply the Angle Bisector Theorem. Also note that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\angle BAD=180^\\circ-BCD=180^\\circ-120^\\circ=60^\\circ</span></p><p>By the Law of Cosines,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">BD^2=AD^2+AB^2-2\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot\\cos\\angle BAD=9^2+24^2-2\\cdot 9\\cdot 24\\cos 60^\\circ=441</span></p><p>so <span class=\"tex\">BD=21</span>. Then, by the Angle Bisector Theorem,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">BF=\\dfrac{AB}{AB+AD}\\cdot BD=\\dfrac 9{9+24}\\cdot 21=\\dfrac{63}{11}</span></p><p>leading to an answer of <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}~\\dfrac{63}{11}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-8"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">w=2+i</span>. What real number <span class=\"tex\">r</span> has the property that <span class=\"tex\">r</span>, <span class=\"tex\">w</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">w^{2}</span> are three collinear points in the complex plane?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{3}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{7}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{3}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{5}{3}</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>We need <span class=\"tex\">2+i</span>,<span class=\"tex\">(2+i)^2 = 3 + 4i</span> and <span class=\"tex\">r</span> to be collinear in the complex plane, which is equivalent to ensuring that the points <span class=\"tex\">(2,1),(3,4),(r,0)</span> are collinear.</p>\n<p>Equating on slopes:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{4-1}{3-2} = \\dfrac{0-4}{r-3} \\rightarrow 3(r-3) = -4 \\rightarrow r = \\dfrac{5}{3} \\rightarrow \n{\\color{OliveGreen}\\boxed{{\\textbf{(E)}}}}.</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-9"}, {"q": "<p> In the figure shown below, major arc <span class=\"tex\">\\widehat{AD}</span> and minor arc <span class=\"tex\">\\widehat{BC}</span> have the same center <span class=\"tex\">O</span>. Also, <span class=\"tex\">A</span> lies between <span class=\"tex\">O</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">D</span> lies between <span class=\"tex\">O</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C</span>. Major arc <span class=\"tex\">\\widehat{AD}</span>, minor arc <span class=\"tex\">\\widehat{BC}</span>, and each of the two segments <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{AB}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{CD}</span> have length <span class=\"tex\">2\\pi</span>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"> <img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2025A/2025_11_05_e415ed6e80d65fa550edg-4.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>What is the distance from <span class=\"tex\">O</span> to <span class=\"tex\">A?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1-\\pi+\\sqrt{1+\\pi^{2}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1+\\pi^{2}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>\nLet <span class=\"tex\">\\angle DOA = \\theta</span> in radians, <span class=\"tex\">OA = r_1</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">OB = r_2</span>. Using <span class=\"tex\">s=r\\theta</span>, we get:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">r_1 (2\\pi - \\theta) = 2\\pi</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">r_2\\theta = 2\\pi</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">r_2-r_1 = 2\\pi</span></p><p>Substituting <span class=\"tex\">r_2 = r_1 + 2\\pi</span> into <span class=\"tex\">r_2\\theta = 2\\pi</span> gives <span class=\"tex\">\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{r_1 + 2\\pi}</span>. Substituting this into the first equation:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">r_1(2\\pi - \\dfrac{2\\pi}{r_1 + 2\\pi}) = 2\\pi \\rightarrow r_1(1 - \\dfrac{1}{r_1 + 2\\pi}) = 1</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">r_1(r_1+2\\pi-1) = r_1 + 2\\pi</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">r_1^2 + (2\\pi-2)r_1 - 2\\pi = 0</span></p><p>By the quadratic formula,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">r_1 = \\dfrac{2-2\\pi \\pm \\sqrt{(2\\pi-2)^2+8\\pi}}{2} = 1-\\pi + \\sqrt{1 + \\pi^2} \\rightarrow\n{\\color{OliveGreen}\\boxed{{\\textbf{(B)}}}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-10"}, {"q": "<p> The orthocenter of a triangle is the concurrent intersection of the three (possibly extended) altitudes. What is the sum of the coordinates of the orthocenter of the triangle whose vertices are <span class=\"tex\">A(2,31)</span>, <span class=\"tex\">B(8,27)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">C(18,27)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">17</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10+4\\sqrt{17}+2\\sqrt{13}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{113}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">54</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>Consider the altitude from <span class=\"tex\">A</span> to <span class=\"tex\">BC</span>. This line has equation <span class=\"tex\">x = 2</span>.</p>\n<p>Consider the altitude from <span class=\"tex\">C</span> to <span class=\"tex\">AB</span>. The slope of this line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of <span class=\"tex\">AB</span>.</p>\n<p>The slope of <span class=\"tex\">AB = \\dfrac{27-31}{8-2} = -\\dfrac{2}{3}</span>, which means the slope of <span class=\"tex\">AB</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{2}</span>.</p>\n<p>Thus the equation of the altitude through <span class=\"tex\">C</span> is:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">y-27 = \\dfrac{3}{2}(x-18)</span> and plugging in <span class=\"tex\">x=2</span> gives <span class=\"tex\">y = 3</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">x+y</span> = <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)~5}}</span><br/></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-11"}, {"q": "<p> The harmonic mean of a collection of numbers is the reciprocal of the arithmetic mean of the reciprocals of the numbers in the collection. For example, the harmonic mean of <span class=\"tex\">4,4,5</span> is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}+\\dfrac{1}{4}+\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)}=\\dfrac{30}{7}.</span></p><p>What is the harmonic mean of all the real roots of the <span class=\"tex\">4050^{\\text{th}}</span> degree polynomial</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\prod_{k=1}^{2025}\\left(kx^{2}-4x-3\\right)=\\left(x^{2}-4x-3\\right)\\left(2x^{2}-4x-3\\right)\\cdots\\left(2025x^{2}-4x-3\\right)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">-\\tfrac{5}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-\\tfrac{3}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-\\tfrac{6}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-\\tfrac{5}{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-\\tfrac{2}{3}</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>\nThe sum of the reciprocals of the roots of <span class=\"tex\">kx^2 - 4x - 3</span> for some <span class=\"tex\">k</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{r_1} + \\dfrac{1}{r_2} = \\dfrac{r_1 + r_2}{r_1r_2} = \\dfrac{\\dfrac{4}{k}}{\\dfrac{-3}{k}} = -\\dfrac{4}{3}</span>.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the sum of all the reciprocals of the roots of the polynomial is <span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{4}{3} * 2025</span> and the average is then <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{-\\dfrac{4}{3}*2025}{4050} = \\dfrac{-2}{3}</span>.</p>\n<p>Reciprocating gives our answer of <span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{3}{2} \\rightarrow \\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}}</span><br/></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-12"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">C=\\{1,2,3,\\ldots,13\\}</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">N</span> be the greatest integer such that there exists a subset of <span class=\"tex\">C</span> with <span class=\"tex\">N</span> elements that does not contain five consecutive integers. Suppose <span class=\"tex\">N</span> integers are chosen at random from <span class=\"tex\">C</span> without replacement. What is the probability that the chosen elements do not include five consecutive integers?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{3}{130}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{3}{143}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{5}{143}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{1}{26}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{5}{78}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>If we skip only one element from <span class=\"tex\">C</span>, then there will be at least <span class=\"tex\">5</span> elements greater than the skipped one or <span class=\"tex\">5</span> elements less.</p>\n<p>Therefore we must skip <span class=\"tex\">2</span> elements and it is easy to see that this is possible e.g. skipping <span class=\"tex\">5</span> and <span class=\"tex\">10</span>.</p>\n<p>Now, we must compute the number of ways to delete <span class=\"tex\">2</span> elements while ensuring no <span class=\"tex\">5</span> numbers are consecutive.</p>\n<p>There are only <span class=\"tex\">3</span> ways to do this - skipping <span class=\"tex\">\\{5,10\\}, \\{4,9\\},</span> or <span class=\"tex\">\\{5,9\\}</span> for a probability of <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{{13 \\choose 2}} =  \\dfrac{1}{26}\\rightarrow\n\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-13"}, {"q": "<p> Points <span class=\"tex\">F, G,</span> and <span class=\"tex\">H</span> are collinear with <span class=\"tex\">G</span> between <span class=\"tex\">F</span> and <span class=\"tex\">H</span>. The ellipse with foci at <span class=\"tex\">G</span> and <span class=\"tex\">H</span> is internally tangent to the ellipse with foci at <span class=\"tex\">F</span> and <span class=\"tex\">G</span>, as shown below.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"> <img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2025A/2025_11_05_e415ed6e80d65fa550edg-5.jpg\" width=\"700\"/></div>\n<p>The two ellipses have the same eccentricity <span class=\"tex\">e</span>, and the ratio of their areas is <span class=\"tex\">2025</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">e?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{3}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{16}{25}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{4}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{22}{23}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{44}{45}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">K</span> be the intersection (or tangency) point of the two circles.</p>\n<p>Note that the ellipses are similar - in fact, consider the dilation at <span class=\"tex\">K</span> that maps the larger ellipse to the smaller ellipse. It must preserve foci, so it must map <span class=\"tex\">F</span> to <span class=\"tex\">G</span> and <span class=\"tex\">G</span> to <span class=\"tex\">H</span>.</p>\n<p>This means that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{FK}{GK}=\\dfrac{GK}{HK}</span></p><p>or <span class=\"tex\">FK\\cdot HK=GK^2</span>.</p>\n<p>If we let <span class=\"tex\">c</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a</span> be defined as in the problem for the larger ellipse, we get <span class=\"tex\">FK=c+a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">GK=c-a</span>.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, since the ellipses are similar, <span class=\"tex\">HK</span> is a scaled down version of <span class=\"tex\">GK</span>, with ratio <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{2025}=45</span> (the ratio of areas of similar figures is the square of the ratio of their lengths).</p>\n<p>Hence, we get</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">FK\\cdot GK\\cdot\\dfrac 1{45}=GK^2</span></p><p>or</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">c+a=45(c-a)</span></p><p>so <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac ca=\\dfrac{22}{23}</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-14"}, {"q": "<p> A set of numbers is called sum-free if whenever <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> are (not necessarily distinct) elements of the set, <span class=\"tex\">x+y</span> is not an element of the set. For example, <span class=\"tex\">\\left\\{1,4,6\\right\\}</span> and the empty set are sum-free, but <span class=\"tex\">\\left\\{2,4,5\\right\\}</span> is not. What is the greatest possible number of elements in a sum-free subset of <span class=\"tex\">\\left\\{1,2,3,\\ldots,20\\right\\}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">11</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Note that there are multiple solutions that lead to a set with size <span class=\"tex\">10</span> - two examples are the odd numbers <span class=\"tex\">\\{1,3,5,\\ldots,19\\}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\{11,12,\\ldots,20\\}</span>.</p>\n<p>We aim to prove that <span class=\"tex\">11</span> elements is impossible. Let the largest element of our subset <span class=\"tex\">S</span> be <span class=\"tex\">k</span>, and consider the ordered pairs <span class=\"tex\">(1, k - 1), (2, k - 2), \\ldots, (\\lfloor \\tfrac{k}{2} \\rfloor, \\lceil \\tfrac{k}{2} \\rceil)</span>.</p>\n<p>Note that both elements of a pair may not be in <span class=\"tex\">S</span>, as they would add to <span class=\"tex\">k</span>. Therefore, we have at most <span class=\"tex\">\\left\\lfloor \\tfrac{k}{2} \\right\\rfloor \\le \\dfrac{20}{2} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)~10}}</span> elements, as desired.</p>\n<p><strong>Remark.</strong> This is a very famous problem in combinatorics, namely the <em>sum-free subset problem</em>. It was proven by Erdős that any set <span class=\"tex\">S</span> of size <span class=\"tex\">n</span> has a sum-free subset of size at least <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{n - 1}{3}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-15"}, {"q": "<p> Triangle <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle ABC</span> has side lengths <span class=\"tex\">AB=80</span>, <span class=\"tex\">BC=45</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">AC=75</span>. The bisector of <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B</span> and the altitude to side <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{AB}</span> intersect at point <span class=\"tex\">P</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">BP?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">18</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">19</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">20</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">21</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">22</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">D</span> be the foot of the altitude from <span class=\"tex\">C</span> to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{AB}</span>, and let <span class=\"tex\">P</span> be the intersection of <span class=\"tex\">BD</span> (the bisector of <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B</span>) with <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{CD}</span>. We find <span class=\"tex\">AD</span> and <span class=\"tex\">BD</span> using the Pythagorean theorem.</p>\n<p>From right triangles <span class=\"tex\">ADC</span> and <span class=\"tex\">BDC</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">AC^{2}=AD^{2}+CD^{2},</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">BC^{2}=BD^{2}+CD^{2}.</span></p><p>From above, we get:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">AC^{2}-BC^{2}=AD^{2}-BD^{2}=(AD-BD)(AD+BD).</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n    AD+BD=AB=80, AC=75, \\, BC=45,&amp;\\implies (75^{2}-45^{2})=80(AD-BD)\\\\\n&amp;\\implies (75-45)(75+45)=80(AD-BD)\\\\\n&amp;\\implies 30\\cdot120=80(AD-BD)\\\\\n&amp;\\implies AD-BD=45.\\\\\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Solving</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{cases}\nAD+BD=80,\\\\\nAD-BD=45,\n\\end{cases}</span></p><p>we get</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">AD=\\dfrac{125}{2}, \\; BD=\\dfrac{35}{2}.</span></p><p>We find <span class=\"tex\">CD</span> by applying the Pythagorean theorem in <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle BDC</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">CD=\\sqrt{BC^{2}-BD^{2}}\n=\\sqrt{45^{2}-\\left(\\dfrac{35}{2}\\right)^{2}}\n=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6875}{4}}\n=\\dfrac{25}{2}\\sqrt{11}.</span></p><p>We use the Angle Bisector Theorem in <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle BCD</span> to find <span class=\"tex\">DP</span>. Since <span class=\"tex\">BP</span> bisects <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{DP}{PC}=\\dfrac{BD}{BC}\n=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{35}{2}}{45}\n=\\dfrac{7}{18}\n\\;\\Rightarrow\\;\nDP=\\dfrac{7}{25} \\, CD.</span></p><p>Hence,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">DP=\\dfrac{7}{25}\\cdot \\dfrac{25}{2}\\sqrt{11}\n=\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{11}}{2}.</span></p><p>Now, we can again use the Pythagorean Theorem in <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle BDP</span> to find <span class=\"tex\">BP</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">BP^{2}=BD^{2}+DP^{2}\n=\\left(\\dfrac{35}{2}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{11}}{2}\\right)^{2}\n=\\dfrac{1225}{4}+\\dfrac{539}{4}\n=\\dfrac{1764}{4}=441.</span></p><p>so <span class=\"tex\">BP={\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)~21}}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-16"}, {"q": "<p> The polynomial <span class=\"tex\">(z+i)(z+2i)(z+3i)+10</span> has three roots in the complex plane. What is the area of the triangle formed by these roots?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">14</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">x = z+2i</span>. Then the polynomial becomes <span class=\"tex\">(x-i)x(x+i)+10 = 0 \\rightarrow x^{3} + x + 10 = 0</span>. We spot that <span class=\"tex\">-2</span> is a root of this polynomial:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">x^{3} + x + 10 = (x+2)(x^{2}-2x+5)</span></p><p>The three solutions for <span class=\"tex\">x</span> are then <span class=\"tex\">-2,1+2i,1-2i</span>. Since shifting the solution back to <span class=\"tex\">z</span> is a translation of the points, we can just compute the area directly. Treating the segment between <span class=\"tex\">(1,2)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(1,-2)</span> as the base and <span class=\"tex\">(-2,0)</span> as the vertex from which we drop the height, we see the area is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A)~6}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-17"}, {"q": "<p> How many ordered triples <span class=\"tex\">(x,y,z)</span> of distinct nonnegative integers <span class=\"tex\">\\le 8</span> satisfy <span class=\"tex\">xy&gt;z,</span> <span class=\"tex\">zx&gt;y,</span> and <span class=\"tex\">yz&gt;x?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">36</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">84</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">186</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">336</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">486</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>Without loss of generality, assume <span class=\"tex\">x &lt; y &lt; z</span>. Now we count the complement, that is how many triples result in <span class=\"tex\">xy \\leq z</span> or <span class=\"tex\">xz \\leq y</span> or <span class=\"tex\">yz \\leq x</span>. <span class=\"tex\">yz \\leq x</span> is impossible and <span class=\"tex\">xz \\leq y</span> only holds if <span class=\"tex\">x = 0</span>, which would imply <span class=\"tex\">xy \\leq z</span> anyways. Therefore, it suffices to compute the number of triples where <span class=\"tex\">xy &lt; z</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">\\textbf{Case 1}</span>: <span class=\"tex\">x = 0</span>. There are <span class=\"tex\">\\binom{8}{2} = 28</span> possibilities for <span class=\"tex\">y</span> and <span class=\"tex\">z</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">\\textbf{Case 2}</span>: <span class=\"tex\">x = 1</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">x = 1</span>, for some given <span class=\"tex\">y</span> then, <span class=\"tex\">z \\in \\{y+1,y+2,\\ldots,y+8\\}</span> all result in <span class=\"tex\">xy &lt; z</span>. There are <span class=\"tex\">8-y</span> options in the set, so summing across all <span class=\"tex\">y</span> gives:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sum_{y=2}^{7} 8-y = 21</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\">\\textbf{Case 3}</span>: <span class=\"tex\">x = 2</span>. We see that <span class=\"tex\">y = 3</span>, <span class=\"tex\">z \\geq 6</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y=4</span>, <span class=\"tex\">z=8</span> work for <span class=\"tex\">4</span> possibilities.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">\\textbf{Case 4}</span>: <span class=\"tex\">x\\geq 3</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">x \\geq 3</span>, then we are guaranteed <span class=\"tex\">xy &gt; z</span>. This gives <span class=\"tex\">0</span> possibilities.</p>\n<p>There are <span class=\"tex\">\\binom{9}{3}</span> ways to pick <span class=\"tex\">x</span>, <span class=\"tex\">y</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">z</span> in increasing order for a total of <span class=\"tex\">6(84-28-21-4) = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)~186}}</span> ordered triples.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-18"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">a,b,c</span> be the roots of <span class=\"tex\">x^{3}+kx+1</span>. What is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a^{3}b^{2}+a^{2}b^{3}+b^{3}c^{2}+b^{2}c^{3}+c^{3}a^{2}+c^{2}a^{3}\\,?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">-k</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-k+1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">k-1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">k</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>We have <span class=\"tex\">a + b + c = 0</span>, <span class=\"tex\">abc = -1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">ab + bc + ac = k</span> from <span class=\"tex\">\\text{Vieta's Formulas}</span>. Factoring adjacent terms, we get the expression equals:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a^2b^2(a+b) + b^2c^2(b+c) + a^2c^2(a+c)</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">=a^2b^2(-c) + b^2c^2(-a) + a^2c^2(-b)</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">= -abc(ab + bc + ac) = \\boxed{\\textbf{(E)~$k$}}</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\">\\textbf{Remark.}</span> The full factorization of the given sum is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;a^{3} b^{2}+a^{2} b^{3}+b^{3} c^{2}+b^{2} c^{3}+c^{3} a^{2}+c^{2} a^{3}\\\\\n&amp;\\quad=(ab+bc+ca)^2(a+b+c)-2abc(a+b+c)^2-abc(ab+bc+ca)\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>In the given problem, the first two terms cancel out as <span class=\"tex\">a+b+c=0</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-19"}, {"q": "<p> The base of the pentahedron shown below is a <span class=\"tex\">13\\times 8</span> rectangle, and its lateral faces are two isosceles triangles with base <span class=\"tex\">8</span> and congruent sides <span class=\"tex\">13</span>, and two isosceles trapezoids with bases <span class=\"tex\">7</span> and <span class=\"tex\">13</span> and nonparallel sides <span class=\"tex\">13</span>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"> <img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2025A/2025_11_05_e415ed6e80d65fa550edg-6.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>What is the volume of the pentahedron?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">416</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">520</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">528</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">676</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">832</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>First, we split the pentahedron into a pyramid and an oblique trianglular prism by drawing parallel lines as follows:</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"> <img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2025A/amc12_20s_a.png\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Consider the pyramid on the left. The distance from the center of the rectangular base to its corners is <span class=\"tex\">5</span>, so its height is <span class=\"tex\">12</span> and its volume is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{3} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 12 = 192</span>. Now, we will transform the oblique triangular prism on the right. Note that the triangular prism formed by the red planes in the diagram below must have the same volume as the original one, since all we have down is shift volume from the right to the left.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"> <img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2025A/amc12_20s_b.png\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>To calculate the volume of this new prism, note that the side lengths are <span class=\"tex\">4\\sqrt{10},4\\sqrt{10},8</span> by the <span class=\"tex\">\\text{Pythogorean theorem}</span>. This has height <span class=\"tex\">12</span> and area <span class=\"tex\">8</span>, so the red triangles have area <span class=\"tex\">48</span>. The height of the prism is <span class=\"tex\">7</span>, and so its volume is <span class=\"tex\">48 \\cdot 7 = 336</span>.</p>\n<p>Hence, the final volume is <span class=\"tex\">192 + 336 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)~528}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-20"}, {"q": "<p> There is a unique ordered triple <span class=\"tex\">(a,k,m)</span> of nonnegative integers such that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{4^{a}+4^{a+k}+4^{a+2k}+\\cdots+4^{a+mk}}{2^{a}+2^{a+k}+2^{a+2k}+\\cdots+2^{a+mk}}=964.</span></p><p>What is <span class=\"tex\">a+k+m?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">11</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>We can use the geometric series formula to write:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{4^{a}+4^{a+k}+4^{a+2 k}+\\cdots+4^{a+m k}}{2^{a}+2^{a+k}+2^{a+2 k}+\\cdots+2^{a+m k}} &amp;= \\dfrac{4^a\\left(\\dfrac{4^{(m + 1)k} - 1}{4^k - 1}\\right)}{2^a\\left(\\dfrac{2^{(m + 1)k} - 1}{2^k - 1}\\right)} \\\\\n&amp;= 2^a \\cdot \\dfrac{2^{(m + 1)k} + 1}{2^k + 1}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2^{(m + 1)k} + 1}{2^k + 1}</span> is odd, so <span class=\"tex\">a = 2</span>, since <span class=\"tex\">964 = 2^{2} \\cdot 241</span>. Hence:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{2^{(m + 1)k} + 1}{2^k + 1} = 241</span></p><p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">241 = 2^{8} - 2^{4} + 1</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">(m + 1)k = 12</span> and <span class=\"tex\">k = 4</span>. Hence, we have <span class=\"tex\">a = 2</span>, <span class=\"tex\">m = 2</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">k = 4</span>, which yields a final answer of <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}~8}</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">\\textbf{Remark.}</span> Note that the last part can be formalized with <span class=\"tex\">\\text{Zsigmondy's Theorem}</span>. <span class=\"tex\">2^{(m+1)k}+1</span> will have a unique prime divisor not dividing <span class=\"tex\">241</span> or <span class=\"tex\">2^{k}+1</span> for any <span class=\"tex\">(m+1)k&gt;12</span>, hence <span class=\"tex\">(m+1)k\\leq 12</span>. It's easy to see <span class=\"tex\">2^{m+1}k\\geq 3\\cdot 241=723</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">(m+1)k=10,11,12</span>, of which only <span class=\"tex\">k=4,m=2</span> works.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-21"}, {"q": "<p> Three real numbers are chosen independently and uniformly at random between <span class=\"tex\">0</span> and <span class=\"tex\">1</span>. What is the probability that the greatest of these three numbers is greater than <span class=\"tex\">2</span> times each of the other two numbers? (If the chosen numbers are <span class=\"tex\">a\\ge b\\ge c</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">a&gt;2b</span>.)</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{1}{12}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{1}{9}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{1}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{1}{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\tfrac{1}{4}</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<div style=\"text-align: center;\"> <img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2025A/problem22amc12.png\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>The region bounded by the planes <span class=\"tex\">z \\geq 2x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">z \\geq 2y</span> has base area <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4}</span> and height <span class=\"tex\">1</span>, so it has volume <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{1}{12}</span> (it is pictured above). The regions where <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> are the greatest have the same volume. Since these regions are disjoint, the total volume is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}~\\dfrac{1}{4}}</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternate Solution</strong></p>\n<p>As before, assume <span class=\"tex\">\\text{WLOG}</span> that <span class=\"tex\">z</span> is the largest. Then, the volume is given by:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\int_0^{1} \\int_{0}^{\\dfrac{z}{2}} \\int_{0}^{\\dfrac{z}{2}} dz \\hspace{.5mm} dx \\hspace{.5mm} dy = \\int_0^{1} \\dfrac{1}{4}z^2 = \\dfrac{1}{12}</span></p><p>Hence, the total volume is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{12} \\cdot 3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}</span>, which yields the same answer as before.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-22"}, {"q": "<p> Call a positive integer fair if no digit is used more than once, it has no <span class=\"tex\">0</span>s, and no digit is adjacent to two greater digits. For example, <span class=\"tex\">196</span>, <span class=\"tex\">23,</span> and <span class=\"tex\">12463</span> are fair, but <span class=\"tex\">1546</span>, <span class=\"tex\">320,</span> and <span class=\"tex\">34321</span> are not fair. How many fair positive integers are there?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">511</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2584</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9841</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">17711</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">19682</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p><span class=\"tex\">\\textbf{Claim.}</span> The smallest digit must appear on one of its ends.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">\\textbf{Proof.}</span> Let <span class=\"tex\">S</span> be a set of digits, and let <span class=\"tex\">s = \\min S</span> denote the smallest element of the set.</p>\n<p>Assume, for the sake of contradiction, that <span class=\"tex\">s</span> is not positioned at either end of a fair arrangement.</p>\n<p>Then <span class=\"tex\">s</span> would have two neighbors, both of which must be greater than <span class=\"tex\">s</span>, since it is the smallest digit in <span class=\"tex\">S</span>.</p>\n<p>This violates the definition of a fair arrangement, which prohibits any digit from being adjacent to two larger digits.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the smallest digit must always occupy one of the end positions. This completes the proof of the claim.</p>\n<p>Once the smallest digit has been placed at an end, we must verify that the remaining digits continue to form a fair arrangement.</p>\n<p>This is achieved by repeating the same logic on the reduced set <span class=\"tex\">S \\setminus \\{s\\}</span>, where we again identify and place the smallest remaining digit at one of the ends.</p>\n<p>To visualize this process, imagine constructing the arrangement step by step.</p>\n<p>Begin with the smallest digit, then sequentially insert each next-smallest digit at either the <strong>left end</strong> or the <strong>right end</strong> of the current sequence.</p>\n<p>If there are <span class=\"tex\">k</span> digits in total, the first digit can be placed in either end position (<span class=\"tex\">2</span> options).</p>\n<p>At each subsequent step, the next-smallest digit can again be placed on either the left or right end.</p>\n<p>This continues until all but one digit is placed, leaving a single remaining position for the final digit.</p>\n<p>Hence, for a set <span class=\"tex\">S</span> with <span class=\"tex\">k</span> digits, the total number of fair arrangements is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">2^{k-1}.</span></p><p>Now, we will count all fair integers. Choose the <span class=\"tex\">k</span> digits (no <span class=\"tex\">0</span>) in <span class=\"tex\">\\binom{9}{k}</span> ways and arrange them fairly in <span class=\"tex\">2^{k-1}</span> ways. Summing over <span class=\"tex\">k=1,\\dots,9</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">T=\\sum_{k=1}^{9}\\binom{9}{k}2^{k-1}\n=\\binom{9}{1}2^{0}+\\binom{9}{2}2^{1}+\\cdots+\\binom{9}{9}2^{8}.</span></p><p>Multiply both sides by <span class=\"tex\">2</span> and add <span class=\"tex\">\\binom{9}{0}</span>:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">2T+\\binom{9}{0}=\\sum_{k=1}^{9}\\binom{9}{k}2^{k}+\\binom{9}{0}\n=\\sum_{k=0}^{9}\\binom{9}{k}2^{k}=(1+2)^{9}=3^{9}.</span></p><p>Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">2T+1=3^{9} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad T=\\dfrac{3^{9}-1}{2}.</span></p><p>Since <span class=\"tex\">3^{9}=19683</span>, the total number of fair positive integers is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">T=\\dfrac{19683-1}{2}=\n\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)~9841}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-23"}, {"q": "<p> A circle of radius <span class=\"tex\">r</span> is surrounded by <span class=\"tex\">12</span> circles of radius <span class=\"tex\">1</span>, externally tangent to the central circle and sequentially tangent to each other, as shown. Then <span class=\"tex\">r</span> can be written as <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b}+c</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">a,b,c</span> are integers. What is <span class=\"tex\">a+b+c?</span></p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"> <img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2025A/2025_11_05_e415ed6e80d65fa550edg-7.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">11</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<div style=\"text-align: center;\"> <img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2025A/problem24.png\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Connect the centers of the smaller circles to form a dodecagon of side length <span class=\"tex\">2</span>. Then, we form a <span class=\"tex\">75^{\\circ}-75^{\\circ}-30^{\\circ}</span> triangle with base <span class=\"tex\">2</span> and legs <span class=\"tex\">r + 1</span>. Hence:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">r + 1 = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin 15^{\\circ}} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)^{-1} = \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}</span></p><p>and so <span class=\"tex\">r = \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2} - 1</span>. Hence, the answer is <span class=\"tex\">6 + 2 - 1 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)~7}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-24"}, {"q": "<p> Polynomials <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">Q(x)</span> each have degree <span class=\"tex\">3</span> and leading coefficient <span class=\"tex\">1</span>, and their roots are all elements of <span class=\"tex\">\\left\\{1,2,3,4,5\\right\\}</span>. The function <span class=\"tex\">f(x)=\\dfrac{P(x)}{Q(x)}</span> has the property that there exist real numbers <span class=\"tex\">a&lt;b&lt;c&lt;d</span> such that the set of all real numbers <span class=\"tex\">x</span> with <span class=\"tex\">f(x)\\le 0</span> consists of the closed interval <span class=\"tex\">[a,b]</span> together with the open interval <span class=\"tex\">(c,d)</span>. How many functions <span class=\"tex\">f(x)</span> are possible?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">11</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">13</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>The solution set of <span class=\"tex\">f(x) \\le 0</span> changes only at roots of <span class=\"tex\">P</span> and <span class=\"tex\">Q</span>. It includes roots of <span class=\"tex\">P</span> that are not roots of <span class=\"tex\">Q</span>, and it never includes roots of <span class=\"tex\">Q</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">f</span> is undefined. To get exactly a closed interval <span class=\"tex\">[a,b]</span> followed by an open interval <span class=\"tex\">(c,d)</span>, <span class=\"tex\">P</span> must supply the closed ends and <span class=\"tex\">Q</span> the open ends.</p><p>Checking every choice of three roots (with repetition) for <span class=\"tex\">P</span> and for <span class=\"tex\">Q</span> from <span class=\"tex\">\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}</span>, exactly <span class=\"tex\">13</span> pairs work:</p><p><span class=\"tex\">(1,2,3)/(1,4,5),\\ (1,2,3)/(3,3,4),\\ (1,2,3)/(3,3,5),\\ (1,2,3)/(3,4,5),\\ (1,2,4)/(3,4,4),\\ (1,2,4)/(4,4,5),\\ (1,2,5)/(3,4,5)</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\">(1,2,5)/(3,5,5),\\ (1,2,5)/(4,5,5),\\ (1,3,4)/(4,4,5),\\ (1,3,5)/(4,5,5),\\ (2,3,4)/(4,4,5),\\ (2,3,5)/(4,5,5)</span></p><p>Here <span class=\"tex\">(1,2,3)/(1,4,5)</span> means <span class=\"tex\">P(x)=(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">Q(x)=(x-1)(x-4)(x-5)</span>. So there are <span class=\"tex\">13</span> possibilities.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2025/part-a/problem-25"}]});
